 "finding": "The campaign did not attack one population of ships. It opened on the largest container ships afloat - seven of the eight hulls sized in 2023-H2 were 300 m or over, median LOA 345 m - and after the box lines rerouted round the Cape in late December 2023 it went on hitting whatever stayed, which was smaller tramp tonnage. The last hit on a 300 m-plus hull in this dataset is Maersk Sentosa on 2024-07-09; 33 attacks follow it, none on a ship that size. Median LOA falls 345 m (2023-H2) to 229 m (2024) to 183 m (2026-H2).",
 "implication_for_the_attack_rate": "The 0.545% per-transit rate in risk-quantification.json divides a numerator that changes composition by a denominator that also changes composition, and neither side is size-controlled. Both the attacks and the Suez transits skew large, so the number is roughly a large-ship rate by accident. But it is an average over two different campaigns - a short one against ULCVs and a long one against sub-panamax tonnage - and quoting it as a single figure hides that.",
